        <h2>Convention and Meeting Management</h2>

        <h3>Convention Service Management</h3>
        <p>For large and complex conventions, convention service managers should assume primary hotel contact person responsibilities <span class="key-point">after the sale is made</span>, not before the meeting planner books the hotel.</p>

        <h3>Meeting Types</h3>
        <p>Corporate meetings include sales meetings, new product meetings, professional/technical meetings, and management meetings. These differ from association, religious, or non-profit meetings in terms of planning approach and objectives.</p>

        <h3>Incentive Meetings</h3>
        <p>Incentive meetings are specifically designed as <span class="key-point">rewards for excellent performance</span> in a company. They differ from other corporate meetings in their purpose and usually offer more premium experiences.</p>

        <h3>Pre-Convention Meetings</h3>
        <p>A pre-convention meeting is held to introduce key hotel personnel to group officials, review the convention agenda, and address detailed arrangements. This meeting is crucial for ensuring all parties are aligned before the event begins.</p>

        <h3>Corporate Meeting Business</h3>
        <p>All other factors being equal, properties with the <span class="key-point">best food service</span> are likely to get the most corporate meeting business. Food quality remains a key differentiator for meeting venues.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Function Rooms Section -->
    <div id="function-rooms" class="topic-container">
        <h2>Function Rooms and Event Space</h2>

        <h3>Function Room Rates</h3>
        <p>Rates for function rooms are determined by multiple factors, including:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Total food and beverage spend</li>
            <li>Number of sleeping rooms booked</li>
            <li>Ceiling height and location of pillars</li>
            <li>Overall space requirements</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Function Room Setup Principles</h3>
        <p>Hotel staff should understand the <span class="key-point">"five feet principle"</span> and <span class="key-point">"two by eight principle"</span> related to the orientation of a screen on a platform in the front of a meeting room and determining seating capacity and screen placement. These guidelines help ensure proper room setup for presentations.</p>

        <h3>Meeting Room Requirements</h3>
        <p>When evaluating meeting spaces, planners should consider whether rooms are free from distractions, as this significantly impacts meeting effectiveness and attendee concentration.</p>

        <h3>Non-Traditional Function Spaces</h3>
        <p>Swimming pool areas can be used as space for cocktail parties, illustrating that areas not traditionally thought of as function rooms can serve as functional event spaces when properly prepared.</p>

        <h3>Function Room Furniture</h3>
        <p>When considering function room furniture, <span class="key-point">flexibility</span> is more important than ease of disposal, handling, or storage. Adaptable furniture allows venues to quickly reconfigure spaces for different event needs.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Food and Beverage Section -->
    <div id="food-beverage" class="topic-container">
        <h2>Food and Beverage Service</h2>

        <h3>Banquet Profitability</h3>
        <p>Banquets are the most profitable area of the Food and Beverage department because of:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Flexibility in pricing</li>
            <li>Higher employee production</li>
            <li>High beverage profits</li>
            <li>Predictable attendance and service requirements</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Banquet Service Styles</h3>
        <p>The most common form of banquet service is <span class="key-point">American/plated service</span>, where pre-plated food is served directly to seated guests. Other options include Russian, English/family style, and buffet service.</p>
        
        <h3>Food and Beverage Billing Components</h3>
        <p>In the Food and Beverage (F&B) industry, the price per person is often denoted as "price, plus, plus" ($++). The two billing components that refer to "plus, plus" are <span class="key-point">tax and service charge</span>. Understanding these components is essential for accurate budgeting and pricing.</p>

        <h3>Food Trends</h3>
        <p>Current food trends in the hospitality industry include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Healthy options</li>
            <li>Lighter options</li>
            <li>Energy-type foods served during breaks</li>
            <li>Accommodations for dietary restrictions</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Beverage Pricing</h3>
        <p>If a group consumes five bottles of gin during a reception and opens one more before it ends, with each bottle costing $35, the total charge will be $210 (6 bottles × $35). Understanding beverage pricing helps in budget planning.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Contracts Section -->
    <div id="contracts" class="topic-container">
        <h2>Contract and Financial Management</h2>

        <h3>Contracts and Letters of Agreement</h3>
        <p>A contract or letter of agreement protects <span class="key-point">both the hotel and the meeting group</span> in case of disputes over services. Clear written agreements prevent misunderstandings about responsibilities and expectations.</p>

        <h3>Standardized Contracts</h3>
        <p>Standardized contracts are criticized by some meeting planners who feel that every event is unique and requires customized terms. However, they are most often used for large meetings due to the longer lead times typically involved.</p>

        <h3>Master Account Billing Authorization</h3>
        <p>The master account billing authorization form, when completed, specifies the limit of financial responsibility a meeting group will accept for its functions. This document is crucial for clarifying payment obligations.</p>

        <h3>Financial Calculations</h3>
        <p>When calculating costs for events, it's important to understand how charges like beverage consumption are determined, usually based on actual consumption rather than estimates.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Association Meetings Section -->
    <div id="association-meetings" class="topic-container">
        <h2>Association Meetings</h2>

        <h3>Association Meeting Planners</h3>
        <p>For association meeting planners, key considerations include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Adequate meeting space</li>
            <li>Sufficient guest rooms</li>
            <li>Adequate exhibit space</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These factors typically outweigh proximity to shopping and entertainment.</p>

        <h3>Association Attendance</h3>
        <p>Attendance at association meetings is generally <span class="key-point">not required</span>. Unlike corporate meetings, association events typically involve voluntary participation by members.</p>

        <h3>Association Revenue Sources</h3>
        <p>Associations earn money from conventions through:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Exhibitions</li>
            <li>Registration fees</li>
            <li>Selling advertisements in convention programs</li>
        </ul>
        <p>However, they typically do not earn money through bed taxes.</p>

        <h3>Similarities Between Markets</h3>
        <p>The nonprofit organization market and the associations meeting market share similarities:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Both have members</li>
            <li>Both are nonprofit</li>
            <li>Both exist to accomplish a purpose</li>
        </ul>
        <p>However, they differ in ease of location through central sources.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Exhibits Section -->
    <div id="exhibits" class="topic-container">
        <h2>Exhibit and Trade Show Management</h2>

        <h3>Exhibit Booth Types</h3>
        <p>An exhibit booth with aisles on all four sides is called an <span class="key-point">island booth</span>. Other booth types include perimeter, standard, and peninsula configurations.</p>

        <h3>Trade Show Management</h3>
        <p>The trade show manager is responsible for developing a list of potential exhibitors, marketing the show to exhibitors and attendees, and contracting with exposition service contractors. This role requires extensive coordination skills.</p>
        
        <h3>Exhibitor Concerns</h3>
        <p>Exhibitors are commonly concerned about how exhibit hours are scheduled. They typically feel that <span class="key-point">delegates should have free time from meetings during the day to browse through the exhibit area</span>. This preference influences exhibition scheduling and impacts overall convention planning.</p>

        <h3>Convention Coordinators</h3>
        <p>The convention coordinator typically assists with servicing small meeting groups and supports the convention service manager. This position requires understanding both hotel operations and meeting planning needs.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Property Analysis Section -->
    <div id="property-analysis" class="topic-container">
        <h2>Property Analysis and Selection</h2>

        <h3>CVB Operations</h3>
        <p>When a Convention and Visitors Bureau (CVB) learns that a group has selected its city for a meeting, it typically sends <span class="key-point">convention lead forms to member hotels</span>. This helps distribute business opportunities among local accommodations.</p>

        <h3>Positive Publicity</h3>
        <p>What lends credibility to a property in the eyes of the public is when the <span class="key-point">source of publicity appears to be an impartial third party</span>. Third-party endorsements carry more weight than self-promotion.</p>

        <h3>Property Analysis Questions</h3>
        <p>When conducting property analyses to attract meeting business, hospitality managers should ask questions about transportation services, meeting room quality, and staff training. The organization chart's simplicity is less critical to meeting planners.</p>
    </div>

